(Title) Tj
T*
(Prophylactic Vacuum-Assisted Wound Closure in Emergency Abdominal Surgery: Study Protocol) Tj
T*
(for a Multicenter Randomized Trial) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Surgical site infection remains a substantial source of postoperative morbidity and) Tj
T*
(healthcare expenditure despite modern aseptic techniques. While negative pressure wound) Tj
T*
(therapy has demonstrated efficacy in managing established wound complications, evidence) Tj
T*
(regarding its prophylactic application in high-risk emergency surgical populations remains) Tj
T*
(limited. This multicenter protocol describes a randomized trial comparing prophylactic) Tj
T*
(negative pressure wound management with standard dressing in patients undergoing emergency) Tj
T*
(abdominal surgery. Ninety participants will be enrolled and randomly assigned to either) Tj
T*
(five days of prophylactic vacuum therapy \(n = 45\) or conventional postoperative wound) Tj
T*
(dressing management \(n = 45\). Primary outcome measures superficial and deep surgical site) Tj
T*
(infections diagnosed through standardized criteria within 30 postoperative days. Secondary) Tj
T*
(measures include cost-effectiveness, wound healing progression, and patient satisfaction.) Tj
T*
(This study addresses whether preventive vacuum application reduces infection risk in this) Tj
T*
(vulnerable surgical population.) Tj
